    "case_id": "723",
    "case_summary": "A 12-year-old castrated Chihuahua presented for periodic health examination. The dog had been diagnosed with tricuspid regurgitation a year prior, which was left untreated for financial reasons. The owner noted that the dog's stool contained parasites, likely nematodes or tapeworms.\n\nInitial examination showed:- Body weight: 4.1 kg- Rectal temperature: 38.3°C - Heart rate: 120 beats/min- Respiratory rate: 30 breaths/min- Heart murmur heard in right heart regionOn follow-up after medication:- Tenderness noted in upper abdomen- Low rectal temperature (36.6°C)- Heart rate 120 beats/min\n\nInitial tests:- Laboratory findings:  * Elevated platelet count (723,000/μL)  * Increased ALT (221 U/L), ALP (159 U/L), γ-GTP (66 U/L)  * Elevated BUN (34.2 mg/dL)  * High total cholesterol (493 mg/dL) and triglycerides (494 mg/dL)  * Elevated phosphorus (5.7 mg/dL) and cystatin C (0.48 mg/dL)  * Spec cPL slightly elevated at 460 μg/dL  * High total bile acid (38.4 μmol/L)  * Negative heartworm antigen testFollow-up tests:- Imaging:  * Thoracic radiography: Enlarged cardiac silhouette  * Abdominal radiography: Hepatomegaly and increased radiopacity in upper right abdomen  * Abdominal ultrasound: Liver enlargement, gallbladder mucocele-like findings, renal cysts, enlarged adrenal glands, reduced duodenal peristalsis, hyperechoic pancreatic surrounding fat- Laboratory findings:  * Elevated ALT (3094 U/L), AST (668 U/L), ALP (311 U/L), γ-GTP (52 U/L)  * High BUN (84.6 mg/dL) and creatinine (1.87 mg/dL)  * Elevated CRP (14.1 mg/dL)  * Low Na (139 mEq/L) and Cl (91 mEq/L)  * High Spec cPL (1202 μg/dL)  * Low urine specific gravity (1.012)",
